
 Minutes of the FIGGY BAR of 26 January 1989

 EXCELLENT FIGGY ! Items discussed: high bit bug in F-PC for Amstrad, F-PC
       Virtual Memory,SOL and ALTAIR revisited, Forth terminal emulation,
       6552 serial transfer, making two Forth boards talk

 Date: 01/26/89   Time: 22:41EST

Attendees:
[[Gary] GARY-S]
[[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN]
[[Len] NMORGENSTERN
[[Kevin] APPERT]
[[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL]
[[milton] M.FABERT] 
       
Minutes:
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> What happened last Sunday? I was watching the superbowl...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> (coming soon to a library near you [when I get it done]...
virtual memory for F-PC)
<[Gary] GARY-S> Evening folks - just got here myself
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Jax helped me solve an archive problem (high bit set in 
unarchived files)
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> & thought I was to go on 6:30!...
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Freudian slip!
<[Gary] GARY-S> was that the source of all your F-PC troubles ?
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Yep. High bit set in some characters in some file names.
<APPERT> is here.
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Dave, what do you mean by virtual memory for F-PC?
<[Gary] GARY-S> I wonder if that is peculiar to the Amstrad or if we need to 
document the bug ????
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> As in...a data set up which allows as much data space 
(assuming you use the defining hooks provided) as you have disk space.
<[Gary] GARY-S> more - if it is true VMS
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Doesn't F-PC have that already?
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> No. It doesn't.
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Thanx
<[Gary] GARY-S> what about the bug ? is it Amstrad specific ?
<[Kevin] APPERT> easy to implement on a system with BLOCK, I use it for the 
code space in assemblers
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Well...it is a sort of cut-rate VMS...for example...the 
first version is not going to incldude garbage collection
<[Kevin] APPERT> and metacompilers
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> I don't know gary.
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> F-PC has block as an option.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> I refuse to use BLOCK. I hate BLOCK. I have a page ALLOTed 
for the virtual memory page.
<K.BUTTERFIEL> is here.
<[Gary] GARY-S> Geeze , Dave... VMS sans garbage collection ? you are going to 
have a pretty full disk darn quick
<[Kevin] APPERT> why do you hate BLOCK, Dave?
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> What is there about your system that is different form 
BLOCK, Dave?
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> You recreate the swap file when you need it.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Just a latent dislike from the days of block oriented 
editors. Furthermore, BLOCK limits page size to 1k.
<[Gary] GARY-S> welcom Ken, you came to a live one !
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> No, it doesn't Dave. you can reset your block size & 
recompile.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Page size in the system I am working on is a maximum of 
64k.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> This doesn't require recompilation.
<[Kevin] APPERT> you swap in and out 64 k at a time?
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> I must admit to a liking for 'normal' files rather then 
blocks
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> No...you swap in and out whenever the virtual address 
needed is not in the current page...and pagesize can be anywhere from 1 byt
e to 64k (user defined)
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Then you are using variable-sized blocks, user defined.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Essentially.
<[Kevin] APPERT> maybe I don't understand, but it sounds like you are working 
WAY to hard at this
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Then why do you hate blocks?
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> If you are using them?
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Because of the way BLOCK is wired into the system.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Kevin -- what do you mean "too hard"?
<[Kevin] APPERT> the goal seems to be ...
<[Kevin] APPERT> a data set which appears to the user (programmer)...
<[Kevin] APPERT> to be in memory, but in fact is on disk...
<[Kevin] APPERT> when not being referenced...
<[Kevin] APPERT> this can be accomplished with less then a (oops) screen of 
code...
<[Kevin] APPERT> why does the user care what the page size is...
<[Kevin] APPERT> when you can accomplish the goal in a very simple, 
straightford way...
<[Kevin] APPERT> so what am I misunderstanding?  I feel I came in in the middle.
..
<[Kevin] APPERT> ( I did!) and I'm missing something
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Well...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> ...for one thing...no single variable can be larger than 
the page size (btw...there is a way around it)...because...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> ...all of the virtual>actual conversion is done when the 
page is loaded,
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> and without an MMU...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> ...it is impossible to trap illegal accesses...
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> pmi what is a MMU?
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> But I am interested in how int is possible to do this in 
one screen?
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> An MMU...
<[Kevin] APPERT> Memory Managemnt Unit
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Thanx
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> He cares about page size when it is fixed because it 
intrudes on programming style.
<[Kevin] APPERT> (usually wears a tie) :)
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> ...is a Memory Management Unit...it (among other things) 
traps references to memory a user does not "own")
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> .
<M.FABERT> is here.
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> This is one reason I like named files and an editor that 
will insert a line without losing another of the bottom of the block.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Ken--have you looked at F-PC?
