<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> This is all done in F-PC (looks like I'll end up doing it 
without using any inline assembly)
<[milton] M.FABERT> VMS As in VAX VMS?
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> I do most of my programming by downloading serially into 
MAX-FORTH systems.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Virtual Memory System
<[Gary] GARY-S> What is your model, Dave ?
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> In which context, Gary?
<[Gary] GARY-S> VMS model
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Well...because I have no way of flagging memory writes, I 
am at the moment assuming pages are always "dirty".
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> Where I have found the VMS to be the most useful is for 
huge data array storage.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Yeah...but because I need a size count for each element, I 
am using a word VCREATE which creates a new "virtual element" complete
 with size count. It can be used...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> ...inside arrays as long as each element is smaller than 
the page size.
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> The old FIG block structure was wonderful for creating an 
array that actually existed on the disk and that was only read into buf
fers when needed.
<[milton] M.FABERT> One nice thing about FORTH is that you can avoid the ...
<[Kevin] APPERT> : virtual@      ( addr --- data )   % 1024 /mod
<[milton] M.FABERT> thrashing inherent in other OS VM systems.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> The idea of what I am doing is avoiding "virtual@" or 
"virtual!"...the idea is to make the VMS as user transparent as possible.
<[Kevin] APPERT>     ( block_offset byte_offset )  swap start_virt + block + @  
;
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> The does> portion of each word defined for virtual memory 
contains the word "virtual>actual", which handles page swapping, and co
nverting the virtual address in the variable to the actual address in the page.
<[Kevin] APPERT> : virtual! ( data addr --- )    1024 /mod swap start_virt + 
block + ! update ;
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> This sounds like the method I used to write virtual arrays.
  The user could treat the array like any other array.  Indexing was d
one by the DOES> clause.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Yeah.
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> How do you handle UPDATE if you are using standard Forth 
blocks?
<[Kevin] APPERT> you have to get a little more clever if you allow things to 
cross page boundarys, but you just have to do the !'s a byte or word at a
 time.
<[Kevin] APPERT> asking me , Len? I don't understand the question
<[Gary] GARY-S> No pointers ?
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> Of course a virtual array is 1/2 the speed of a memory 
array because of the UPDATE in each store.
<[Kevin] APPERT> bye the way, : % decimal  ; immediate
<[milton] M.FABERT> Update should be done when you do a Virtual! or you run the 
...
<[Kevin] APPERT> it's a lot slower than that!  Ken
<[milton] M.FABERT> risk of overwritting the block in a multitasking system ...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> The updating scheme I am using doesn't update the swap 
file until the page needs to be changed.
<[Kevin] APPERT> the disk drive isn't used until the BLOCK buffer is needed in 
forth, it's
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Isn't that risky Dave? It puts you at the mercy of a power 
failure.
<[Kevin] APPERT> just marked as changed by UPDATE, Dave.
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> Depending on the algorithm accessing the data you might 
spend most of the time in local parts of the array and only seldom have t
o swap pages.
<[Kevin] APPERT> your have to FLUSH when you a done
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Disk I/O is just as risky, Len. A power fialure can crash 
a hard drive, etc etc... As it hahappens, I have batteries anyway.
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> What I mean is that if there is a long time between writes.
..
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> the risk is increased.
<[Kevin] APPERT> you have to mark your pages as "dirty" (changed) somehow, Dave,
 that's all UPDATE is
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> But the swapfile is only for use while the program is 
running. A power failure would render the swapfile useless anyway.
<[Ken] K.BUTTERFIEL> Multitasking gives fits to most FORTH systems UNLESS each 
task has its own block buffer region pointed to by a user variable!!!!
<[Kevin] APPERT> if it's just data, it's usually expendable anyway
<[Len] NMORGENSTERN> Kevin, that's a programmer talking, not a user!
<[Kevin] APPERT> It came from somewhere, Len
<[milton] M.FABERT> Within an application, if you do not UPDATE and access 
BLOCK ...
